WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.710
Transportation is access.

2
00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:07.662
It connects people to work, education, food, care, family, recreation, and public life.

3
00:00:07.662 --> 00:00:09.851
Transportation is also exposure.

4
00:00:09.851 --> 00:00:17.376
Routes and projects distribute traffic danger, pollution, noise, cost, travel time, and displacement pressure.

5
00:00:17.376 --> 00:00:23.123
This episode adapts Kristine Miller's Transportation Equity chapter under CC BY 4.0.

6
00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:26.817
The narration condenses and paraphrases Miller's work.

7
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:32.427
It excludes third-party reports, statistics, images, quotations, and linked media.

8
00:00:32.427 --> 00:00:37.398
Evaluate a trip from door to destination, not one vehicle or facility in isolation.

9
00:00:37.398 --> 00:00:41.232
A bus route is not accessible if the path to the stop is unsafe.

10
00:00:41.232 --> 00:00:45.903
A short distance is not convenient if a traveler must make multiple transfers.

11
00:00:45.903 --> 00:00:51.354
A bicycle facility is not useful to everyone if gaps occur at the most dangerous locations.

12
00:00:51.354 --> 00:00:56.445
Use six tests: affordability, safety, reliability, accessibility, time, and exposure.

13
00:00:56.445 --> 00:00:59.559
Apply them to different travelers and trip purposes.

14
00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:08.064
Include people who do not drive, people with disabilities, older adults, youth, shift workers, caregivers, and households with limited income.

15
00:01:08.064 --> 00:01:10.101
Do not treat any group as uniform.

16
00:01:10.101 --> 00:01:13.994
Transportation benefits and burdens can fall in different places.

17
00:01:13.994 --> 00:01:19.864
A project that saves drivers time may increase crossing distance or exposure for nearby residents.

18
00:01:19.864 --> 00:01:23.098
State the tradeoff instead of hiding it in an average.

19
00:01:23.098 --> 00:01:24.479
Build an access table.

20
00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:26.363
Select essential destinations.

21
00:01:26.363 --> 00:01:34.400
Estimate total time, money, transfers, walking or rolling distance, schedule constraints, and failure risk for each travel mode.

22
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.742
Add qualitative evidence about comfort, harassment, shade, seating, wayfinding, and perceived safety.

23
00:01:40.742 --> 00:01:44.258
Engagement must reach riders where and when they travel.

24
00:01:44.258 --> 00:01:48.528
A meeting held after service ends excludes the people most affected.

25
00:01:48.528 --> 00:01:53.300
Report back with route or design changes and explain unresolved constraints.

26
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.975
Torrance application segment.

27
00:01:54.975 --> 00:01:58.209
This original segment is separate from Miller's chapter.

28
00:01:58.209 --> 00:02:04.447
Its cited local source is the official Torrance Transit website, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

29
00:02:04.447 --> 00:02:09.587
Routes, fares, and schedules can change, so current service information must be verified.

30
00:02:09.587 --> 00:02:14.496
Choose a hypothetical trip between a Torrance home area and an essential destination.

31
00:02:14.496 --> 00:02:18.828
Compare driving, transit, walking or rolling, and bicycling where feasible.

32
00:02:18.828 --> 00:02:23.795
Include first and last segments, transfer risk, operating hours, and accessible paths.

33
00:02:23.795 --> 00:02:29.109
Repeat the comparison for a worker with a late shift and a traveler using a mobility device.

34
00:02:29.109 --> 00:02:33.902
Identify one infrastructure change, one service change, and one information change.

35
00:02:33.902 --> 00:02:36.155
Name which agency controls each action.

36
00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:41.526
This is a study method, not a finding about Torrance Transit performance or legal compliance.

37
00:02:41.526 --> 00:02:47.991
Transportation equity asks who can get where, under what conditions, at what cost, and with which burdens.

38
00:02:47.991 --> 00:02:52.321
The answer should be measured at the scale of a person's complete trip.

39
00:02:52.321 --> 00:02:55.736
This adaptation credits Kristine Miller under CC BY 4.0.

40
00:02:55.736 --> 00:03:01.225
It excludes third-party chapter material and adds a separately cited Torrance application.

41
00:03:01.225 --> 00:03:02.811
No endorsement is implied.
